Psychology is the scientific study of behavior and mental processes. Psychologists study how people think, feel, learn, remember, develop, and interact with others. 
Psychology includes many different areas of study. Biological psychology examines how the brain, nervous system, hormones, and genetics influence behavior. 
Cognitive psychology studies mental processes such as attention, memory, problem solving, and decision making. 
Developmental psychology examines how people change physically, cognitively, emotionally, and socially throughout their lives. 
Social psychology studies how people's thoughts, feelings, and behaviors are influenced by other people. 
Personality psychology examines patterns of thoughts, feelings, and behaviors that make individuals different from one another. 
Clinical psychology focuses on understanding and treating mental, emotional, and behavioral difficulties. Psychologists use scientific methods to study behavior. 
Common research methods include experiments, surveys, interviews, observations, and correlational studies. 
An experiment allows researchers to examine cause-and-effect relationships by manipulating an independent variable and measuring its effect on a dependent variable. 
A control group may be used as a comparison with an experimental group. A correlation describes a relationship between two variables. 
A positive correlation means that two variables tend to increase or decrease together. A negative correlation means that as one variable increases, the other tends to decrease. 
Correlation does not by itself demonstrate that one variable causes another. Psychological research also involves ethical principles. 
Researchers should protect participants from unnecessary harm, obtain informed consent when appropriate, protect private information, and explain the study's purpose and procedures. 
Psychology connects to many other fields, including biology, education, medicine, sociology, neuroscience, and law.
